{
  "image_name": "43c699c95250b847dddc162cf8ec13dc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how materials can be represented by increasingly detailed “fingerprints” for data-driven modeling, moving from coarse descriptors to fine-grained atomic environments. It spans different material types on the left, including 2D lattices, 3D crystals, and polymer-like chains, suggesting the scheme is broadly applicable across material classes.\n\nThe main comparison is between three families of features. At the coarsest level, property-based features describe a material using bulk or tabulated information such as elemental identity, atomic properties, electrical and thermal behavior, lattice parameters, and other physical quantities. A more detailed middle level uses fragment-based features, where the material is broken into recurring atomic or structural motifs and represented by the fractions of these fragments or blocks, along with related morphological descriptors. The most detailed level uses atomic-position-based features, which encode pairwise distances and angular relationships through basis-function methods such as SOAP, AGNI, and MTP.\n\nThe figure also highlights graph-based representations, where atoms and bonds form a network from which local patterns are extracted, plus other alternatives like Coulomb matrices and electron density. Overall, it conveys a hierarchy of material descriptors with increasing structural resolution.",
